    content: "Every NFL dynasty is built in April. The draft is where teams acquire the core talent that sustains success, and the best franchises treat it like a science. The foundation of any winning draft strategy is quarterback: teams that believe they have found their franchise signal-caller will move heaven and earth to get him, trading multiple first round picks to move up the board. The quarterbacks who enter the league with elite traits get graded on a different scale than any other position, and their contract value under the NFL salary cap makes them the most important asset a team owns. Beyond the quarterback, successful franchises draft with an eye on scheme fit. A defensive coordinator running a 3-4 base needs edge rushers who can drop into coverage, while a zone-heavy offense wants receivers with sharp route running. The teams that win the draft are the ones who resist picking for need and instead trust their draft grade, taking the best player available even when it creates a position battle in training camp. Late-round value is where championships are financed: finding a starter in round five or six gives a team a cost-controlled contributor for four years, freeing cap space to sign veterans in free agency. Rookie rankings mean little on draft night; what matters is how the pieces fit the roster, the depth chart and the coaching staff. The 2026 season will show which front offices built their winners in April.",
    imageUrl: "https://sc02.alicdn.com/kf/A61edb853f76541cc92fec9fc0c198535d.png",
