    id: 10015,
    title: "How Defensive Coordinators Attack Modern Quarterbacks",
    slug: "how-defensive-coordinators-attack-modern-quarterbacks",
    excerpt: "Blitz percentages, disguised coverages, simulated pressure — how NFL defensive coordinators scheme against modern quarterbacks.",
    content: "Defensive coordinators have spent the last decade chasing the modern quarterback, and the arms race is only getting faster. The spread, RPO and motion-heavy offenses of 2026 force defenses to be more creative than ever, disguising coverage until the snap and rotating safeties to create false reads for young quarterbacks. The most effective weapon is simulated pressure: showing blitz, dropping the would-be rusher into coverage, and sending someone the offense did not account for. Elite coordinators marry their blitz packages to their personnel, using the depth chart to manufacture matchups on the back end. The result is a chess game played at 20 miles per hour, where one disguised coverage call can flip a division game. The 2026 season will reward the defensive staffs that best mix pressure and deception, and the quarterback rankings at year's end will reflect it. Keep an eye on the safeties — the modern positional hybrids who make those scheme looks work — and the injury report, because depth decides whether a blitz-heavy scheme survives November.",
    imageUrl: "https://sc02.alicdn.com/kf/Ae73d7bcf506a4abcbb8a7bb069feebe7x.png",
